\newpage
\begin{verbatim}
% RULE RExclam
< m1: SENTENCE{SENTENCErec1}[mu1] 
>

< m : SENTENCE{SENTENCErec1}[mu1, 
                             I2:sig1 ]
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I2: ALL (sig1, puncrel/PUNC(AUX_exclamPUNCkey){PUNCrec2}) 
           OR
           ALL (sig1, puncrel/PUNC(AUX_periodPUNCkey){PUNCrec1})
      >

<
   SUBRULE (* 1. there is an exclamation mark in analysis *)
      < >
      < sig1: [puncrel/PUNC(AUX_exclamPUNCkey){PUNCrec3}] >

      COMP
         <
          C1: SENTENCErec1.senttype = mainclause AND
              SENTENCErec1.mood = imperativemood
          A1: @
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: SENTENCErec1.senttype = mainclause AND
              SENTENCErec1.mood IN [imperativemood, letsmood]
          A1: @
         >

   SUBRULE (* 2. no exlamation mark in analysis *)
      < >
      < sig1: [] >

     (* no COMP part *)

      DECOMP
         <
          C1: SENTENCErec1.senttype = mainclause AND
              SENTENCErec1.mood IN [imperativemood, letsmood]
          A1: @
         >

   SUBRULE (* 3. there is a period; only for letsmood *)
      < >
      < sig1: [puncrel/PUNC(AUX_periodPUNCkey){PUNCrec3}] >

      COMP
         <
          C1: SENTENCErec1.senttype = mainclause AND
              SENTENCErec1.mood = letsmood
          A1: @
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: SENTENCErec1.senttype = mainclause AND
              SENTENCErec1.mood = letsmood
          A1: @
         >
>
&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] RExclam
\item[* relevant examples:] Go home! Let's go home. (Anal.\ only:) Let's go 
home!
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] clauseTOsentence
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) RC\_Punc
\item[* date of creation:] 10-JUN-1988
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] Dutch Rpunc2
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 21/02/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders\\
Adapted for letsmood
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] to introduce an exclamation mark after commands, and a period 
after letsmood sentences.
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals) The letsmood 
sentences receive their period here instead of in RPeriod, because Dutch and 
Spanish assume that all letsmoods are real imperatives (they cannot take a 
period). Hence, either the IL mapping of the punctuation rules in English must 
allow for Rperiod to map ont LExclam, or RExclam must be adapted. The latter 
option was chosen. It is assumed that in analysis, a letsmood may take an 
exclamation mark.
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:] One subrule for the 
case there is an exclamation mark in analysis, one subrule for the case there 
isn't; one subrule for letsmood periods.
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:] in analysis, no exclamation 
mark need be present; for letsmoods, an exclamation mark may be present in 
analysis.
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item problems:\\
See RPeriod
\item modifications:\\

\end{enumerate}
\end{description}

\newpage
\begin{verbatim}
% RULE RPeriod
< m1: SENTENCE{SENTENCErec1}[mu1] 
>

< m : SENTENCE{SENTENCErec1}[mu1, 
                             I2:sig1 ]
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
        I2: ALL (sig1, puncrel/PUNC(AUX_periodPUNCkey){PUNCrec2})       
      >

<
   SUBRULE (* 1. There is a period in analysis *)
      < >
      < sig1: [puncrel/PUNC(AUX_periodPUNCkey){PUNCrec3}] >

      COMP
         <
          C1: SENTENCErec1.senttype = mainclause AND
              SENTENCErec1.mood = declarative
          A1: @
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: SENTENCErec1.senttype = mainclause AND
              SENTENCErec1.mood = declarative
          A1: @
         >

   SUBRULE (* 2. no period in analysis *)
      < >
      < sig1: [] >

      (* no COMP part *)

      DECOMP
         <
          C1: SENTENCErec1.senttype = mainclause AND
              SENTENCErec1.mood = declarative
          A1: @
         >
>
&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] RPeriod
\item[* relevant examples:] That was enough for today. ; \ \ I'll ask my friends 
in the E.E.C..
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] Clausetosentence
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) RC\_Punc
\item[* date of creation:] 10-JUN-1988 
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] Dutch Rpunc3
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 21/02/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders\\
Removed letsmood sentences from this subrule. They are dealt with by RExclam 
now.
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] to introduce a period at the end of declarative sentences
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:] one subrule for 
the case there is a period in analysis, one for the case there isn't
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:] in analysis, no period 
need be present
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\
It is assumed that periods going with abbreviations are part of the word, not 
separate puncrels. Therefore, it's impossible to check for such a period in the 
M-rules. If double periods (see example above) are considered unacceptable, a 
special rule has to written for the generative lay-out component to remove one.
\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item problems:\\
Not yet solved: absence of punctuation when we are dealing with a sentence-
final embedded sentence in quotation marks (He said: 
`I'll go home early today.' \ \ He said: `Dare you call me a liar?')
\item modifications:\\

\end{enumerate}
\end{description}

\newpage
\begin{verbatim}
% TRANSFORMATION TCommaIncorp
< m1: SENTENCE{SENTENCErec1}
              [ I1:leftdislocrel/N1
                             [ mu1, 
                               puncrel/PUNC(KEY_commaPUNCkey){PUNCrec1}
                             ],
                mu2
              ]
>

< m : SENTENCE{SENTENCErec1}
              [ I1:leftdislocrel/N1[mu1],
                puncrel/PUNC(KEY_commaPUNCkey){PUNCrec1},
                mu2
              ]
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I1: N1.CAT IN [SENTENCE] OR
           ( N1.CAT IN [PREPP] AND
             EXIST (mu1, [objrel/I11::NP{NPrec1}[mu3]]) )
       I11: NPrec1.NPhead IN [sentNP, openIngNP]
      >

      COMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] TCommaIncorp
\item[* relevant examples:] [Before he left] , he closed the door $\rightarrow$ 
[Before he left,] he closed the door
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] CLAUSEtoSENTENCE
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) TC\_CommaIncorp
\item[* date of creation:] 15-MAR-1989 
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] Dutch Tpopldlcomma
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] To incorporate (analytically speaking) the comma in the 
(leftdislocrel) embedded clause; this is needed because it is doubtful whether 
the grammar can deal with an initial comma in all the rules between here and 
the ConjSent Substitution rules (where the comma is deleted). The Surface 
Parser is not capable of putting the comma in the embedded clause straight away
(ask Jan O. why...)
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:]
